From 7878a1c413bfbd46b783d52e38f7104582933d73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tyler Baker <tyler.baker@oss.qualcomm.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 13:47:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] clk: qcom: gcc-sa8775p: keep the QUP SE clock rates at
 registration

The QUP serial engine RCGs use clk_rcg2_shared_ops, whose init parks
an RCG on the XO safe source when the clock controller registers it.
That is harmless for the serial engines Linux drives, whose drivers set
their rate before use, but it breaks a serial engine that another
exception level owns and that firmware set up. Under Xen the debug UART
(QUP1 SE3) is the hypervisor console and is hidden from the hardware
domain; once GCC registers in dom0, gcc_qupv3_wrap1_s3_clk_src runs
from XO, the UART baud generator is off and every later console byte
is garbage. On the Lemans EVK the console turns to noise right after

  [    5.719058] Found an unknown type of DRAM info struct (size = 216)

in every dom0 boot. Keeping GCC from probing in dom0 (by disabling the
RPMh power domain provider it depends on) keeps the console intact,
while keeping the CX vote, protecting the UART's branch clock and
disabling the whole QUP1 wrapper do not; the parked RCG sits behind the
branch clock.

Use clk_rcg2_shared_no_init_park_ops for the QUP serial engine RCGs,
as gcc-eliza, gcc-glymur and gcc-kaanapali do, so registration leaves
the rate that firmware programmed. With this change dom0 boots to a
login prompt on the Xen console.

Signed-off-by: Tyler Baker <tyler.baker@oss.qualcomm.com>
